notes.body:
In #13388 from packet 90 to 91 we are jumping the equivalent of 18 packets, found later (129 - 163), the first being marked as OOO, and the others as Retransmissions. I would say they're all retransmissions, that's what is saying this !7424, but it's not the point anyway.

I could have it working by modifying packet-tcp.c this way :
```
     if (reassemble_ooo && tcpd && !(tcpd->fwd->flags & TCP_FLOW_REASSEMBLE_UNTIL_FIN)) {
-        if (!PINFO_FD_VISITED(pinfo)) {
+        if (!PINFO_FD_VISITED(pinfo)&&0) {
             /* If there is a gap between this segment and any previous ones
              * (that is, seqno is larger than the maximum expected seqno), then
              * it is possibly an out-of-order segment. The very first segment
```

and either using !2445 (turning all Retransmissions to OOO manually) or commenting the RETRANSMISSION part (packet-tcp.c again) :
```
-            if((tcpd->ta->flags&TCP_A_RETRANSMISSION) == TCP_A_RETRANSMISSION) {
+            /*if((tcpd->ta->flags&TCP_A_RETRANSMISSION) == TCP_A_RETRANSMISSION) {
                 const char* str = "Retransmitted ";
                 nbytes = tvb_reported_length_remaining(tvb, offset);
                 proto_tree_add_bytes_format(tcp_tree, hf_tcp_segment_data, tvb, offset,
                     nbytes, NULL, "%sTCP segment data (%u byte%s)", str, nbytes,
                     plurality(nbytes, "", "s"));
                 goto clean_exit;
-            }
+            }*/
```
These 2 methods are equivalent and confirm that Retranmissions are 1/ not taken into account (nothing new), and 2/ the real problem of #13388 is in the reassembly.

Further investigation is required to understand why breaking the test !PINFO_FD_VISITED(pinfo) makes it work, I don't see any obvious reason to this right now.

